+**Test restore** (0.126.0+) proves a backup can be brought back: the
+backup's newest snapshot is restored with `restic restore --verify` (every
+restored file is read back and compared with the repository) into a new
+temporary volume `debcontrol-verify-<id>`, which is removed however the
+job ends. Nothing the containers use is touched; the machine needs room
+for one more copy of the backup while it runs. The result - files and
+bytes restored out of how many the snapshot holds - is shown on the
+backup (`DockerBackup.last_verify_*`). It fails when a file is missing or
+restic reports an error, and a failed test restore fires *Docker backup
+failed*. It can be scheduled (*Docker backup: test restore*). Audited as
+`machine.docker.backup.verify`.
+
 **Importing an existing repository** is two steps: create a backup with
 that repository's address and password and tick *Show every snapshot in
 the repository*, then restore its snapshots with *Restore into...*.
